WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.720
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:16.000
Today is going to be all about turbulence! And we&nbsp;
are going to do this, and this, and this and…this.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.480
To understand what this new paper has to offer,&nbsp;
we have to look at an earlier work named Wavelet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:28.080
Turbulence. Apart from the fact that it in&nbsp;
my opinion, it is one of the best papers ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:35.280
written, it could do one thing extremely well.&nbsp;
In goes a coarse, low-resolution smoke or fluid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:42.080
simulation, and out comes a proper simulation&nbsp;
with a lot more details. And, it is typically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:49.840
accurate enough to fool us. And all this was&nbsp;
possible in 2008. Kind of boggles the mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:56.800
so much so, that this work even won a technical&nbsp;
Oscar award. Please remember this accuracy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:03.520
statement as it will matter a great deal.&nbsp;
Once again, accurate enough to fool us.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:09.120
Now, more than a decade later, here we are,&nbsp;
this new method is leaps and bounds better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:13.440
and can do 5 amazing things that&nbsp;
the previous methods couldn’t do.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:21.520
One, it can do spatiotemporal upsampling.&nbsp;
Upsampling both in space and in time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:27.840
What does this mean? It means that in&nbsp;
goes a choppy, low resolution simulation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:35.600
and out comes a smooth, detailed simulation.&nbsp;
Wow, now that is incredible. It is really able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:42.240
to fill in the information not only&nbsp;
in space, but in time too. So good.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:50.640
Now, two, previous methods typically try to take&nbsp;
this coarse input simulation and add something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:58.640
to it. But not this new one. This new method&nbsp;
creates a fundamentally new simulation from it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:03.680
Just look here. It didn’t just add a few&nbsp;
more details to the input simulation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:08.320
this is a completely new work.&nbsp;
That is quite a difference.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:16.400
Three, I mentioned that Wavelet Turbulence&nbsp;
was accurate enough to fool us. So I wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:22.880
how accurate this new method is? Well, that’s&nbsp;
what we are here for, so…let’s see together!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:30.080
Here comes the choppy input simulation, and here&nbsp;
is an upsampling technique from a bit more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:38.560
a year ago. Well, better, yes, but the output is&nbsp;
not smooth, I could characterize it more as less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:47.840
choppy. And, let’s see the new method. Can it do&nbsp;
any better? My goodness. Look at that! That is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:55.360
smooth and creamy animation with tons of details.&nbsp;
Now, let’s pop the question - how does it compare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:03.920
to the reference simulation - reality if you will?
What? I cannot believe my eyes. I can’t tell the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:10.320
difference at all! So this new technique is&nbsp;
not just accurate enough to fool the human eye,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:15.760
this is accurate enough to stand up to&nbsp;
the real, high-resolution simulation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:21.280
And all this improvement in just&nbsp;
one year. What a time to be alive!

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:29.280
But wait a second. Does this even make sense?&nbsp;
If we have the real reference simulation here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:35.440
why do we need the upsampling technique? Why&nbsp;
not just use the reference? Well, it makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:42.800
sense - the plan is that we only need to compute&nbsp;
the cheap coarse simulation, upsample it quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:48.640
and hope that it is as good as the reference&nbsp;
simulation, which takes a great deal longer.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:57.440
Well, okay, but how much longer? Now, hold on&nbsp;
to your papers, and let’s see the results, and….&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:04.720
yes! This is about 5 to 8 times faster than&nbsp;
creating the high-resolution simulation we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:11.200
compared to, which is absolutely amazing,&nbsp;
especially that it was created with a modern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:15.280
blazing fast reference simulator&nbsp;
that runs on your graphics card.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:25.280
But, remember, I promised you five advantages, not&nbsp;
three. So, what are the remaining two? Well, four,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:30.080
it can perform compression! Meaning&nbsp;
that the output simulation will take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:38.400
up to 600 times less data on our disk, that is&nbsp;
insanity. So, how much worse is the simulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:47.360
stored this way? My goodness, look at that! It&nbsp;
looks nearly the same as the original one. Wow.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.320
And, we are still not done yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:57.440
Not even close! Five. It also works on super&nbsp;
high Reynolds numbers. In other words, if we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:02.000
some of the more difficult cases&nbsp;
where there is tons of turbulence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:07.040
it will works really well. This typically&nbsp;
gives a lot of troubles to previous techniques.

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:14.800
Now, one more important thing. Views are, of&nbsp;
course, not everything. However, I couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:25.920
not notice that this work was only seen by&nbsp;
127 people. Yes. I am not kidding. 127 people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:30.640
This is why I am worried that if we don’t&nbsp;
talk about it here on Two Minute Papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:36.000
almost no one will talk about it. And&nbsp;
these works are so good, people have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:41.200
to know! Thank you very much for watching&nbsp;
this, and let’s spread the word together!

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:33.200
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

